    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently looking at the best way to catalog a new acquisition of rare 19th-century manuscripts for our university collection here in Belgium. Could you provide a comparison between the Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC) and the Library of Congress Classification (LCC) systems? I'm specifically interested in how they handle metadata, scalability for large collections, and the level of granularity they offer for specialized historical documents.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC) is known for its intuitive numerical structure and is often simpler for general public access, while Library of Congress Classification (LCC) offers much finer, alphanumeric granularity suitable for highly specialized academic archives. For metadata, both systems provide classification points, but LCC's extensive classification schedules often support more nuanced cataloging fields for rare materials. Considering the specialized nature of 19th-century manuscripts, LCC usually offers superior depth and scalability for your detailed historical indexing needs.",
      "source": "target",
